metalive wrote:
> Douglas Henke wrote:

>> and taking risks you should avoid or defer.

> Does this mean accumulating tons and tons of unknown scroll and
> potions in my inventory, getting burdened by doing so, only hoping to
> soon find an entire library filled with identification scrolls
> "later" on?!?

I count being burdened as a risk you should avoid or defer.

Leave scrolls and potions in a box somewhere, and return when you've
found a means to identify them. Alternatively, you may test potions by
first dipping an item into them (to check for polymorph), then testing
them with a unicorn horn, finally quaffing them (it is unwise to try
potions out without having a unicorn horn). Some potions will become
obvious by monster behaviour (throwing harmful potions at you or
quaffing beneficial ones will identify them for you, if you can see them
do it). If you kill a nymph, the potion she carries is almost always
object detection.

Personally, I never try out unidentified scrolls. A few are
auto-identified when monsters use them (create monster or teleportation,
for example), and teleport is the one you may find in a niche just
behind a wall).

As always in NetHack, patience is the key.

